WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.129
Your speaker notes are written for you, not for a listener.

2
00:00:04.179 --> 00:00:08.530
Here's how to turn rough notes into a voiceover that sounds clear and natural.

3
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.863
Rough notes work on stage, but a voice needs full sentences.

4
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:19.494
Instead of 'results, three groups, control flat', say: let's look at the results.

5
00:00:19.544 --> 00:00:21.284
We tested three groups.

6
00:00:21.784 --> 00:00:26.647
A real script tells the viewer where to look, and links smoothly to the next slide.

7
00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:29.623
Write the way you speak.

8
00:00:29.673 --> 00:00:31.620
'Utilise' becomes 'use'.

9
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.965
'In order to' becomes 'to'.

10
00:00:34.015 --> 00:00:38.506
Keep one idea per sentence, because a listener can't scan back up the page.

11
00:00:39.006 --> 00:00:42.625
Abbreviations, symbols and numbers can come out oddly.

12
00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:50.162
So 3.5% becomes three and a half per cent, and n=240 becomes two hundred and forty participants.

13
00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:53.363
Narration takes its cues from punctuation.

14
00:00:53.413 --> 00:00:55.616
A full stop is a short pause.

15
00:00:55.666 --> 00:00:57.404
A comma, a smaller one.

16
00:00:57.454 --> 00:00:58.750
There was one exception.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.296
Low-light images.

18
00:01:01.062 --> 00:01:06.318
Write full, short sentences, spell numbers as words, and read it aloud once.

19
00:01:06.368 --> 00:01:10.440
On Pro, a.i. directs the video, timing each scene to your words.

20
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.727
Try Basic first at powerpoint2vid.com.

